---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="About | Liberating Scripture Collective"
  description="The Liberating Scripture Collective is a nonprofit organization building open-access scripture translation, teaching, and community centered on liberation, healing, and belonging."
  canonical="https://liberatingscripture.org/about/"
>
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <script
      type="application/ld+json"
      is:inline
      set:html={JSON.stringify({
        "@context": "https://schema.org",
        "@graph": [
          {
            "@type": "AboutPage",
            "@id": "https://liberatingscripture.org/about/#webpage",
            url: "https://liberatingscripture.org/about/",
            name: "About | Liberating Scripture Collective",
            isPartOf: { "@id": "https://liberatingscripture.org/#website" },
            about: { "@id": "https://liberatingscripture.org/#organization" },
          },
          {
            "@type": "Person",
            "@id": "https://liberatingscripture.org/about/#brandon",
            name: "Brandon C. Vélez Johnson",
            jobTitle: "Bible Translator and Mental Health Counselor",
            worksFor: { "@id": "https://liberatingscripture.org/#organization" },
            url: "https://litbible.net/about",
          },
        ],
      })}
    />
  </Fragment>
  <div class="about-page">
    <header class="about-hero">
      <div class="about-hero__inner content">
        <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
        <h1>The Liberating Scripture Collective</h1>
        <p class="lede about-hero__lede">
          An organization devoted to reading the Christian scriptures with
          honesty about power, trauma, liberation, and human dignity.
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="about-body content">
      <section class="about-section" aria-labelledby="about-org">
        <h2 id="about-org">What we are</h2>
        <p>
          The Liberating Scripture Collective is a registered 501(c)(3) nonprofit
          organization founded by Brandon Vélez Johnson
          to support the
          <a
            href="/lit-bible/">Liberation and Inclusion Translation</a
          >
          and build the wider ecosystem around it.
        </p>
        <p>
          We create open-access, trauma-informed translation, learning
          resources, and pathways of spiritual support that serve liberation,
          healing, and belonging. The Collective brings together Bible
          translation, teaching, public conversation, and spiritual
          accompaniment, shaped by the conviction that scripture should free
          people, not dominate and demean them.
        </p>
        <p>
          The "Collective" in our name is intentional. This isn't about
          authorities telling people what to believe. It's a hub for "spiritual
          mutual aid": people coming together to build, learn, question, and
          accompany one another.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="about-section" aria-labelledby="about-values">
        <h2 id="about-values">What we believe</h2>

        <h3>Scripture should liberate, not control.</h3>
        <p>
          The Bible has a long history of being used to control, exclude, and
          harm. That's not a peripheral problem to work around; it's the central
          one. Every choice LSC makes starts with the conviction that scripture
          is liberating, and using it to do anything else is a betrayal of what
          it is.
        </p>

        <h3>
          The people this work centers are those who have been most betrayed by
          tradition.
        </h3>
        <p>
          Most Bible resources are built for people who are already comfortable
          with scripture, people who have experienced it as comforting and
          empowering. LSC doesn't assume that has been the case for everyone.
          The person who has been hurt, excluded, or scapegoated is who the work
          centers. Centering them doesn't exclude anyone else. It just refuses
          to treat them as an afterthought.
        </p>

        <h3>
          We are honest about the harm so we can heal it instead of add to it.
        </h3>
        <p>
          Scripture has caused enough damage that ignoring that history is its
          own kind of choice. LSC takes seriously what harmful interpretations
          of scripture have done to people and thinks carefully about every
          translation decision, how teaching is framed, how community is held,
          and how the organization makes decisions, knowing that our convictions
          have to be lived to mean anything.
        </p>

        <h3>Everyone belongs. Full stop.</h3>
        <p>
          Belonging isn't something people have to earn or prove. Human dignity
          isn't a reward for right belief. It's where LSC starts because it's
          where we believe the Bible starts, without exception. When someone
          causes harm or excludes others, protection and accountability are
          necessary. But even then, the core belonging persists.
        </p>

        <h3>Open access is a theological commitment, not just a policy.</h3>
        <p>
          Keeping LSC's resources free isn't just a nice idea; it's a
          theological commitment. Scripture and the tools to understand it have
          historically been controlled by institutions, professionals, and those
          with money. Scripture has always been on the side of the oppressed, so
          the work stays open because radical inclusion is the gospel itself.
        </p>

        <h3>
          How we treat people in the work is inseparable from what the work is
          for.
        </h3>
        <p>
          You can't build something oriented toward liberation and belonging
          while treating the people who build it as expendable. Care, dignity,
          and equity aren't values LSC holds about scripture and then abandons
          in practice. The Collective tries to be what it's building toward.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="about-section" aria-labelledby="about-relationship">
        <h2 id="about-relationship">This site and litbible.net</h2>
        <p>
          <strong
            ><a
              href="https://litbible.net"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener noreferrer">litbible.net</a
            ></strong
          >
          is where the translation lives. Sometime in 2027, it will include all 260
          chapters of the New Testament, thoroughly annotated, fully searchable, and
          regularly updated. It also hosts the
          <a
            href="https://litbible.net/found-in-translation-podcast"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer">Found in Translation podcast</a
          >, the translation commitments, the glossary, and articles.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>liberatingscripture.org</strong> is the Collective's home. This
          is where you'll find information about the Collective itself, its other
          projects, its governance, how to get involved, and how to support the work.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="about-section" aria-labelledby="about-governance">
        <h2 id="about-governance">Governance</h2>
        <p>
          The Collective operates with a consensus-based governance model.
          Brandon Vélez Johnson is the founding steward who does the translation
          work and collaborates with the board to make organizational decisions.
          The intent is for the Collective to be genuinely shared, accountable,
          and not dependent on any single person's vision or authority.
        </p>
        <p>
          LSC is in active development as an organization. This structure will
          continue to be shaped by the people who join the work.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="about-section" aria-labelledby="about-brandon">
        <h2 id="about-brandon">About the founder</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>Brandon C. Vélez Johnson</strong> is a Bible translator and mental
          health counselor who began the Liberation and Inclusion Translation in 2020.
          His work is grounded in the conviction that how we translate scripture shapes
          how people experience God, themselves, and each other, and that those choices
          have real consequences for real people.
        </p>
        <p>
          For the full story, visit the
          <a
            href="https://litbible.net/about"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer">About page on litbible.net</a
          >.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="about-section" aria-labelledby="about-involved">
        <h2 id="about-involved">Get involved</h2>
        <p>
          The Collective is built by people, not just supported by them. If you
          want to contribute, whether through feedback, collaboration,
          scholarship, accessibility work, art, spiritual accompaniment, or
          simply showing up in the community, there is room.
        </p>
        <div class="about-actions">
          <a href="/contact/" class="btn"><span>Contact us</span></a>
          <a href="/support/" class="btn btn--outline"
            ><span>Support the work</span></a
          >
          <a href="/community/" class="btn btn--outline"
            ><span>Community &amp; Courses</span></a
          >
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .about-hero {
    background: var(--green-deep);
    padding: clamp(3.5rem, 7vw, 6rem) 0;
  }

  .about-hero__inner {
    max-width: calc(64 * var(--ch));
  }

  .about-hero h1 {
    font-family: "Crimson Text", Georgia, serif;
    font-size: clamp(2.2rem, 4.5vw, 3.8rem);
    color: var(--cream);
    margin: 0.75rem 0 1.25rem;
  }

  .about-hero .eyebrow {
    color: var(--cream);
  }

  .about-hero .lede {
    color: var(--cream);
  }

  .about-body {
    padding: clamp(3rem, 6vw, 5rem) 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 3.5rem;
  }

  .about-section {
    max-width: calc(64 * var(--ch));
    margin-inline: auto;
  }

  .about-section h2 {
    font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 2.5vw, 2rem);
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
    padding-bottom: 0.5rem;
    border-bottom: 2px solid var(--green);
    display: inline-block;
  }

  .about-section h3 {
    font-size: clamp(1.4rem, 2vw, 1.5rem);
    font-weight: 600;
    margin: 2rem 0 0.4rem;
  }

  .about-section h3:first-of-type {
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }

  .about-section p {
    max-width: calc(68 * var(--ch));
  }

  .about-actions {
    display: flex;
    gap: 1rem;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }

  .about-actions .btn {
    padding: 10px 20px;
  }
</style>
